2198,"On the authority of ʿAbdullāh ibn Masʿūd, he said: The chiefs of Quraysh passed by the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him and his family) while with him were Ṣuhayb, Bilāl, ʿAmmār, Khabbāb, and others like them from the weak Muslims. They said: ""O Muḥammad! Drive them away. Are you pleased with these from your people? Shall we be followers of these?! Are these the ones upon whom Allah has favored from among us?! Perhaps if you drive them away, we will come to you!"" So there was revealed: ""And do not drive away those who call upon their Lord morning and evening, seeking His Face. You are not accountable for them in anything, nor are they accountable for you in anything, that you should drive them away and thus be among the wrongdoers."" (Al-Anʿām: 52)"
2199,"On the authority of Ibn ‘Abbas, who said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ passed away [from this world], and he had appointed Abu Ruhm Kulthum ibn al-Husayn al-Ghifari as his successor over Medina. He set out for ten nights remaining in Ramadan. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ fasted, and the people fasted with him until, when he was at Al-Kadid — between ‘Usfan and ‘Amj — he broke his fast. Then he proceeded until he alighted at Marr al-Zahran with ten thousand Muslims from Muzaynah and Sulaym, and in every tribe there were numbers and Islam. The Emigrants (Muhajirun) and the Helpers (Ansar) accompanied the Messenger of Allah ﷺ in full force; not a single one of them stayed behind.

When the Messenger of Allah ﷺ alighted at Marr al-Zahran, the news had been cut off from Quraysh; no news of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ reached them, and they did not know what he was going to do. On that night, Abu Sufyan ibn Harb, Hakim ibn Hizam, and Budayl ibn Warqa’ went out, scouting and waiting to see if they could find any news or hear of it.

Al-‘Abbas ibn ‘Abd al-Muttalib had already come to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ along the way. Also, Abu Sufyan ibn al-Harith ibn ‘Abd al-Muttalib and ‘Abdullah ibn Abi Umayyah ibn al-Mughirah had met the Messenger of Allah ﷺ between Mecca and Medina. They sought permission to enter upon him. Umm Salamah spoke to him concerning them, saying: ""O Messenger of Allah! Your cousin, your aunt’s son, and your brother-in-law."" He said: ""I have no need for them. As for my cousin, he violated my honor; and as for my aunt’s son and my brother-in-law, he is the one who said to me in Mecca what he said.""

When this reply was conveyed to them, and Abu Sufyan had a young son with him, he said: ""By Allah, he will surely give me permission, or I will take this son of mine by the hand, and we will wander the earth until we die of thirst and hunger."" When this reached the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, he felt compassion for them, then gave them permission. They entered and embraced Islam.

When the Messenger of Allah ﷺ alighted at Marr al-Zahran, Al-‘Abbas said: ""Morning has come for Quraysh! By Allah, if the Messenger of Allah ﷺ enters Mecca by force before they seek quarter, it will be the destruction of Quraysh forever."" He said: ""So I sat on the Messenger of Allah’s ﷺ white mule and rode out on it until I reached Al-Arak. I said: 'Perhaps I will meet some woodcutters, or a milk carrier, or someone with a need who will go to Mecca and inform them of the Messenger of Allah’s ﷺ location, so they may come out to him and seek quarter before he enters it upon them by force.' By Allah, I was traveling on it, seeking what I had gone out for, when I heard the speech of Abu Sufyan and Budayl ibn Warqa’, as they conversed with one another. Abu Sufyan was saying: 'I have never seen fires or an army like this day.' Budayl said: 'These are, by Allah, the fires of Khuza’ah, stirred up by war.' Abu Sufyan said: 'Khuza’ah, by Allah, are too humiliated and base for these to be their fires and their army.'

I recognized his voice, so I said: 'O Abu Hanzalah!' He recognized my voice and said: 'Abu al-Fadl?' I said: 'Yes.' He said: 'What is the matter, may my father and mother be ransomed for you?' I said: 'Woe to you, O Abu Sufyan! This is the Messenger of Allah ﷺ among the people. Morning has come for Quraysh, by Allah!' He said: 'What is the remedy, may my father and mother be ransomed for you?' I said: 'By Allah, if he captures you, he will surely strike your neck. Ride behind me on this mule until I bring you to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ to seek quarter for you.' So he rode behind me, and his two companions returned.

Whenever I passed a fire of the Muslims, they would say: 'Who is this?' When they saw the Messenger of Allah’s ﷺ mule, they would say: 'The uncle of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ on his mule.' Until I passed the fire of ‘Umar ibn al-Khattab (may Allah be pleased with him). He said: 'Who is this?' and stood up toward me. When he saw Abu Sufyan on the crupper of the camel, he said: 'Abu Sufyan? The enemy of Allah? Praise be to Allah Who has given you power over him without a contract or covenant.' Then he rushed off toward the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, and I spurred the mule. It outpaced him by the measure that a swift beast outpaces a slow man. I dismounted from the mule and entered upon the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, and ‘Umar entered. He said: 'O Messenger of Allah! This is Abu Sufyan; Allah has given you power over him without a contract or covenant. Let me strike his neck.' I said: 'O Messenger of Allah, I have [already] granted him protection.' Then I sat with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, took hold of his [Abu Sufyan’s] head, and said: 'By Allah, no man shall converse privately with him tonight except me.'

When ‘Umar persisted in his regard, I said: 'Gently, O ‘Umar! By Allah, if he were from the men of Banu ‘Adi ibn Ka’b, you would not have said this. But you know he is a man from the men of Banu ‘Abd Manaf.' He said: 'Gently, O ‘Abbas! By Allah, your Islam on the day you embraced Islam was dearer to me than the Islam of al-Khattab would have been had he embraced Islam. And that is only because I knew that your Islam was dearer to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ than the Islam of al-Khattab [had he embraced Islam].'

The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: 'Take him to your quarters, O ‘Abbas! When morning comes, bring him to me.' So I took him to my quarters, and he spent the night with me. When morning came, I went out with him to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. When the Messenger of Allah ﷺ saw him, he said: 'Woe to you, O Abu Sufyan! Has the time not come for you to know that there is no deity but Allah?' He said: 'May my father and mother be ransomed for you! How generous, forbearing, and kind you are! By Allah, I thought that if there were another with Allah, he would have availed me something [by now].' He said: 'Woe to you, O Abu Sufyan! Has the time not come for you to know that I am the Messenger of Allah?' He said: 'May my father and mother be ransomed for you! How forbearing, generous, and kind you are! By Allah, there was still something in my soul concerning this until now.'

Al-‘Abbas said: 'Woe to you, O Abu Sufyan! Embrace Islam and testify that there is no deity but Allah and that Muhammad is the Messenger of Allah before your neck is struck.' So he testified with the testimony of truth and embraced Islam.

I said: 'O Messenger of Allah! Abu Sufyan is a man who loves this pride/honor; so grant him something.' He said: 'Yes. Whoever enters the house of Abu Sufyan is safe; whoever closes his door is safe; and whoever enters the Mosque is safe.'

When he turned to leave, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: 'O ‘Abbas! Detain him at the narrow pass of the valley by the mountain’s edge, until the soldiers of Allah pass by him so he may see them.' So I went out with him and detained him where the Messenger of Allah ﷺ had commanded me.

The tribes passed by him on their banners. Every time a tribe passed, he would say: 'Who are these?' I would say: 'Sulaym.' He would say: 'What have I to do with Sulaym?' Then another tribe would pass. He would say: 'Who are these?' I would say: 'Muzaynah.' He would say: 'What have I to do with Muzaynah?' Until the tribes had all passed. No tribe passed but he would say: 'Who are these?' I would say: 'Banu so-and-so.' He would say: 'What have I to do with Banu so-and-so?'

Until the Messenger of Allah ﷺ passed in the Green Battalion (Al-Khadra’), in which were the Emigrants and the Helpers. Nothing of them could be seen but their eyes. He said: 'Glory be to Allah! Who are these, O ‘Abbas?!' I said: 'This is the Messenger of Allah ﷺ among the Emigrants and the Helpers.' He said: 'No one has any power or strength against these. By Allah, O Abu al-Fadl! The kingdom of your brother’s son has become great this morning.' I said: 'O Abu Sufyan! This is Prophethood.' He said: 'Then yes, indeed.'

I said: 'Go to your people.' So he went out until, when he came to them, he cried out at the top of his voice: 'O people of Quraysh! This is Muhammad, come to you with what you cannot resist. Whoever enters the house of Abu Sufyan is safe.' His wife, Hind bint ‘Utbah, stood up to him, seized his moustache, and said: 'Kill the greasy, fat one! What a wretched scout for a people!' He said: 'Woe to you! Do not let this woman deceive you from yourselves. He has come with what you cannot resist. Whoever enters the house of Abu Sufyan is safe.' They said: 'Woe to you! What good is your house?' He said: 'And whoever closes his door is safe, and whoever enters the Mosque is safe.' So the people dispersed to their homes and to the Mosque."
